---
/**
 * Starlight `Footer` override.
 *
 * Starlight's stock Footer does two unrelated jobs: it draws the site footer
 * chrome, and it draws the per-page article affordances (edit link, last-updated
 * stamp, prev/next pagination). This site moved the footer chrome out to
 * PageFrame.astro, outside `.main-frame`, so it spans the full page width
 * instead of being inset to one document's content column.
 *
 * That move emptied this override entirely, which threw the per-page
 * affordances out along with the chrome. The tell is in starlight-custom.css:
 * it carries a `.pagination-links a` rule styling prev/next in brand red, for
 * markup that had not rendered since. So this file exists to put back the part
 * that belongs to the article, and nothing else.
 *
 * `LastUpdated` is deliberately absent. It reads git committer timestamps, and
 * every file under src/content/docs shares the single Jekyll-to-Astro migration
 * commit's date, so it would print one identical wrong date on every page.
 * `lastUpdated` is correspondingly left unset in astro.config.mjs, with the
 * same reasoning recorded there. Revisit once the docs have real edit history.
 *
 * Imported by concrete path rather than through the `virtual:starlight/components/*`
 * specifiers that Starlight's own Footer uses. Those resolve a configured user
 * override if one exists, which would be the more composable choice, but they are
 * only declared in `@astrojs/starlight/virtual-internal.d.ts`, which the public
 * `virtual.d.ts` does not reference. So `astro check` cannot type them (ts2307),
 * and since `npm run build` is `astro check && astro build`, using them breaks the
 * build. Reaching into an internal declaration file to fix that would be worse.
 *
 * The two are equivalent here: the `components` map in astro.config.mjs overrides
 * only Header, Footer and PageFrame, so there is no EditLink or Pagination
 * override for the virtual specifier to find. If one is ever added, this file has
 * to import it directly, because these concrete paths will not pick it up.
 */
import EditLink from '@astrojs/starlight/components/EditLink.astro';
import Pagination from '@astrojs/starlight/components/Pagination.astro';
---

<footer class="sl-flex">
  <div class="meta sl-flex">
    <EditLink />
  </div>
  <Pagination />
</footer>

<style>
  /* Not inside `@layer starlight.core`: this is site CSS overriding Starlight,
     not a reimplementation of its layer, so it should win without a
     specificity contest. Matches how starlight-custom.css is written. */
  footer {
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-5, 24px);
  }

  /* `.meta` keeps Starlight's class name so its stock rules still apply, but
     the spacing above it comes from our scale rather than Starlight's 3rem, so
     the gap between the article's last paragraph and the edit link reads the
     same as the other vertical rhythm on the page. */
  .meta {
    gap: 12px 48px;
    justify-content: space-between;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: var(--space-6, 32px);
    font-size: var(--text-body-sm, 14px);
    color: var(--nr-text-muted);
  }

  /* A single child (the edit link, with no last-updated stamp beside it) would
     otherwise be pushed to the inline end by Starlight's own
     `.meta > p:only-child { margin-inline-start: auto }`. The edit link should
     sit on the content's left edge, on the same x as the prose above it. */
  .meta > :global(*:only-child) {
    margin-inline-start: 0;
  }
</style>
